title The merge that never conflicted, and two rows that derived above a published spine
prompt The baton, unattended -- the tree opened mid-rebase, one command done, four living pins unmerged.
think The tree was not idle. An interactive rebase stood mid-conflict replaying f577798a0 onto 52d911c28, with CHECKPOINTS, ITINERARY, REDS and the session-logs pin all UU. Reading the state came before touching anything.
think REDS was the consequential one and it was the derived spine's own shape. This commit booked %416 and %417 from a local read; the anointed spine had meanwhile published %416 at stamp 072118 and %417 at 070821 -- different stamps, same numbers, which is a squat on both.
think Fetch-before-book refused: git fetch xy died on host key verification inside the jail. The ref xy/main was already present and resolved to 52d911c288, which is the exact commit this rebase replays onto, so the spine read is the anointed one and the failed fetch cost nothing. Named rather than assumed.
think The instrument agreed with the read before I moved a byte: next_free 421, double_booked %416 and %417. A published number never moves and only unshared rows renumber, so 20260905.070617 took %421 and 20260905.071847 took %422, earlier stamp taking the lower number. Their citations swept with them -- two inside %422's own body, one in the archive shelf, two in the 070617 log.
think The other three pins resolved by their own rules rather than by preference. CHECKPOINTS is append-only, so both cairns stand, newest first by stamp. The session-logs shelf table is a counted table, so I counted: 20260903 reads 65 rows and 20260831 reads 26, which is HEAD's side and not mine -- my counts were stale. ITINERARY took upstream's condensed card whole, since upstream had already folded what my side still carried long.
think Then the find. A uniq -d over every shelf's stamp column returned 20260904.095307 twice, two rows for one log, each describing it differently. Git had merged that file CLEANLY -- no conflict, no marker -- because two different lines added at one position is a textually clean merge, and only the stamp inside them says they are one row. Base held zero, each side added one, the merge kept both.
think The guard reads past it by named rule. index_row_bound_scan.sh's own header says a CLOSED shelf passes free, and it watches the living pin plus the open shelf only. Run on metal with the duplicate standing, the witness printed GREEN across thirty-one behaviors. That is %381's failure mode surviving on any shelf that is not the newest, so it booked %423 -- and the repair kept the published row and lifted the unshared one, the same boundary the derived spine draws.
obs A conflict marker is a guard that fires when two hands disagree about content at one position. Here the two hands agreed on the position and disagreed on the content, which is exactly the disagreement no marker reports -- so the safest-looking merge in the round was the one carrying the fault. The sweep afterward reads zero duplicates across all 44 shelves, so the population was one and it was born in this rebase.
obs The lap cannot land, and the reason is %422 measured again rather than recalled. The identity half has since been fixed -- user.name and user.email are both set now. The signing half is not: commit.gpgsign is true, gpg.program names /home/keeper/grain-petrichor/.gnupg-rye/gpg.sh, and this host holds exactly one tree, grain-pheromone. No gpg.sh, no .gnupg-rye, no private-keys-v1.d, no agent socket, no gpg.format fallback anywhere under /home/keeper. Two independent blockers, both custody.
obs Scope held where the law said stop. %360 is 6,359 bytes and the obvious fold candidate, and it is OPEN -- a BOOKED row may fold, an OPEN row stays -- so the ledger's overage waits for a hand rather than for my judgment. The overage is also this ledger's normal breathing: 60776bcad stood at 28,644 against the same 24,576 and folded back two commits later.
obs The riscv witness reds and it is not a regression. The base commit asserts the identical line 55 emu_seva.ok, and qemu-riscv64-static is absent on this pier, so it refuses for want of an instrument. Its header makes the emulated run its whole claim, where the mobile witness's header always promised the cross-build as a provable half -- which is why only the mobile one gained the presence probe, and why leaving the sibling alone is a reading rather than an oversight.
loom witness=reds_ledger_monotone measured=423 span=423 remainder=403 disagreements=0
loom witness=reds_spine_derive rebindings=0 squatters=0 double_booked=0 next_free=424
loom shelves_swept=44 duplicate_stamps_before=1 duplicate_stamps_after=0
loom pin_bytes=ITINERARY:32762/32768 REDS:33182/24576
file construction/REDS.md rows %416 and %417 renumbered to %421 and %422 above the published spine, %423 booked for the duplicate shelf row, census re-derived to 423/403 and proven by the monotone witness on metal
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md both cairns kept, newest first, my 071847 cairn's row citations swept to %421 and %422
file construction/ITINERARY.md upstream's condensed card taken whole, Git nib set to 52d911c288 which is HEAD's parent after this rebase, the %420 account's census corrected to 422/402 then 423/403, one line added naming all three of this round's rows
file session-logs/README.md shelf counts resolved to the measured values, 65 for 20260903 and 26 for 20260831
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260904.md the duplicate row for 20260904.095307 lifted, published row kept
file session-logs/date/20260905/20260905-070617_the-wall-that-read-a-filename.kyri citations swept to %421, headline arithmetic corrected to 420 to 422
file construction/archive/20260905-071847_itinerary-settled-decisions.md one citation swept to %422
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- GATES-ONLY. Every conflict is resolved, every guard green, the index holds exactly its set, and the rebase stands one signed commit from done. This tree cannot sign: gpg.program points at a peer ship's tree that does not exist here and no key, agent, or fallback is reachable. A hand with the key runs git rebase --continue; nothing else is left in this lane.
